===== README.md
     1	# Smart Code Review
     2	
     3	Smart Code Review combines a small Order Management microservice with a repository-local,
     4	Markdown-driven Solution Architect review model. The service is the demonstration codebase;
     5	`AGENTS.md`, `.agents/skills/code-review`, and `./code-review` contain the review standards and
     6	automation.
     7	
     8	## Technology
     9	
    10	- Java 23
    11	- Spring Boot 4.1
    12	- Gradle 9.6 with the Gradle Wrapper
    13	- Spring Web, Spring Data JPA, Spring Security, Bean Validation, Flyway, and Actuator
    14	- H2 for local demonstrations and PostgreSQL for the production profile
    15	- JUnit 5, Mockito, AssertJ, and MockMvc
    16	
    17	## Prerequisites
    18	
    19	Install the following before running the application or its code review:
    20	
    21	- Git
    22	- JDK 23
    23	- A shell capable of running Bash scripts (`Terminal` on macOS/Linux, or Git Bash/WSL on Windows)
    24	- [OpenAI Codex CLI](https://developers.openai.com/codex/cli), authenticated with an account that
    25	  can use Codex
    26	
    27	Install Codex on macOS or Linux using the current official installer:
    28	
    29	```shell
    30	curl -fsSL https://chatgpt.com/codex/install.sh | sh
    31	```
    32	
    33	Open a new terminal, then verify the installation and authentication:
    34	
    35	```shell
    36	codex --version
    37	codex login status
    38	```
    39	
    40	If authentication is required, run `codex login` and complete the sign-in flow. Never commit or
    41	share an API key, access token, or Codex credential with this repository.
    42	
    43	Verify that Gradle can select the required Java 23 toolchain:
    44	
    45	```shell
    46	java -version
    47	./gradlew --version
    48	```
    49	
    50	## Clone and verify the project
    51	
    52	```shell
    53	git clone https://github.com/gunturmanjusha/SmartCodeReview.git
    54	cd SmartCodeReview
    55	./gradlew clean test
    56	./gradlew build
    57	./gradlew jacocoTestCoverageVerification jacocoTestReport
    58	```
    59	
    60	The Gradle Wrapper downloads the repository's supported Gradle version when necessary, so a
    61	separate Gradle installation is not required. The build selects Java 23 and enforces at least 85%
    62	line coverage and 80% branch coverage.
    63	
    64	Open the human-readable JaCoCo coverage report after the build:
    65	
    66	```shell
    67	# macOS
    68	open build/reports/jacoco/test/html/index.html
    69	
    70	# Linux
    71	xdg-open build/reports/jacoco/test/html/index.html
    72	
    73	# Windows PowerShell
    74	start build/reports/jacoco/test/html/index.html
    75	```
    76	
    77	## Run a Solution Architect code review
    78	
    79	Run commands from the repository root. The simplest command selects the review scope from the
    80	current branch:
    81	
    82	```shell
    83	./code-review
    84	```
    85	
    86	- On `main`, it reviews the complete repository.
    87	- On a feature branch, it reviews only files added, modified, renamed, or deleted by that branch,
    88	  while inspecting minimal directly affected unchanged code when context is necessary.
    89	- Feature branches are compared with local `main`, or with `origin/main` when local `main` is
    90	  unavailable.
    91	- Outside Git or at a detached `HEAD`, automatic review stops because no current branch is available.
    92	
    93	Automatic branch comparison requires committed feature changes and a clean working tree. This keeps
    94	the tested revision identical to the reviewed revision. Use staged mode when the work is not ready
    95	to commit.
    96	
    97	The launcher performs the following work automatically:
    98	
    99	1. Runs the clean Gradle test suite.
   100	2. Runs the complete Gradle build.
   101	3. Enforces the JaCoCo coverage gates and generates its HTML report.
   102	4. Invokes Codex as a read-only senior Solution Architect reviewer.
   103	5. Evaluates every applicable review control without changing application code.
   104	6. Prints the review in the terminal and generates Markdown and HTML reports.
   105	
   106	The evidence model covers seven solution-level categories:
   107	
   108	- Architecture and Design
   109	- Code Quality and Maintainability
   110	- API and Integration Design
   111	- Security and Data Protection
   112	- Reliability and Operational Readiness
   113	- Data and Persistence
   114	- Testing and Verification
   115	
   116	### Open the code-review report
   117	
   118	The main architect-facing result is:
   119	
   120	```text
   121	build/reports/code-review/latest.html
   122	```
   123	
   124	Open it with:
   125	
   126	```shell
   127	# macOS
   128	open build/reports/code-review/latest.html
   129	
   130	# Linux
   131	xdg-open build/reports/code-review/latest.html
   132	
   133	# Windows PowerShell
   134	start build/reports/code-review/latest.html
   135	```
   136	
   137	The report leads with the decision and a color-coded executive assessment that identifies the
   138	Spring Boot Order Management REST microservice, the verified flaw count, the highest risk, and the
   139	developer and architect actions. A **Findings and recommended corrections** table then shows the
   140	category, severity, owner, repository evidence, technical impact, and recommended correction for
   141	every failed or partial result. Issue links jump directly to the detailed evidence and verification.
   142	Related implementation and testing symptoms are grouped into one root-cause issue instead of
   143	appearing as duplicate flaws. Each developer-owned issue includes a short Java, SQL, configuration,
   144	or pseudocode fix sketch tied to the exact class or method, plus the focused test to run. Full source
   145	files and unified diffs are deliberately omitted from the architect report. Architect-owned issues
   146	identify the repository evidence, required decision, recommended option, and tradeoff without
   147	inventing missing business or platform requirements. The
   148	report also shows the evidenced business flow and solution architecture, seven-category scorecard,
   149	compact verification summary with a clickable JaCoCo HTML report, and a clear separation between
   150	routine developer corrections and decisions requiring architect judgment. Every verified problem
   151	includes its control, classification, severity, exact Java file and line or method, repository
   152	evidence, failure scenario, technical impact, recommended correction, confidence, and verification
   153	command. Passing controls remain
   154	compact so architects can focus on material decisions.
   155	
   156	Every root cause is labelled as an **Architecture flaw** or **Developer code flaw**. Independently
   157	actionable defects remain separate even when they appear in the same Java class, so architects can
   158	validate boundary and contract corrections while developers receive precise implementation and
   159	regression-test guidance.
   160	
   161	### Review scopes
   162	
   163	| Command | Scope | When to use it |
   164	| --- | --- | --- |
   165	| `./code-review` | Automatically selected from branch | Review the complete repository on `main`; on a feature branch, review only its changes against `main`. |
   166	| `./code-review --repository` | Complete current repository | Explicitly review the whole repository from any branch. |
   167	| `./code-review --staged` | Staged Git changes | Review changes added with `git add` before committing. |
   168	| `./code-review main` | Current branch compared with `main` | Explicitly select `main` as the comparison base. |
   169	| `./code-review origin/main` | Current branch compared with `origin/main` | Compare with the locally available remote-tracking baseline. |
   170	
   171	For branch comparison, the launcher identifies and displays the currently checked-out feature
   172	branch. When an explicit argument is used, `main` or `origin/main` is the **base revision**, not the
   173	feature branch name. The base revision must already exist locally; the launcher does not fetch or
   174	change Git state. Unchanged files may be inspected only for directly affected context; unrelated
   175	pre-existing code is outside the review scope. The full build and test suite still runs to detect
   176	regressions caused by the feature.
   177	
   178	### Feature-branch review workflow
   179	
   180	```shell
   181	git switch main
   182	git pull
   183	git switch -c feature/your-change
   184	
   185	# Implement and test the feature.
   186	./gradlew clean test
   187	
   188	# Commit the feature so branch-comparison mode can review it through HEAD.
   189	git add .
   190	git commit -m "Describe the feature"
   191	
   192	# The checked-out feature branch and main comparison base are detected automatically.
   193	./code-review
   194	
   195	# macOS: open the architect report.
   196	open build/reports/code-review/latest.html
   197	```
   198	
   199	To review work before committing, stage the intended files and use staged mode:
   200	
   201	```shell
   202	git add path/to/changed/files
   203	./code-review --staged
   204	open build/reports/code-review/latest.html
   205	```
   206	
   207	### Generated reports
   208	
   209	| Path | Purpose |
   210	| --- | --- |
   211	| `build/reports/code-review/latest.html` | Final, readable architect report from the most recent review. |
   212	| `build/reports/code-review/latest.md` | Markdown source for the same review. |
   213	| `build/reports/code-review/code-review-<timestamp>.html` | Timestamped HTML audit copy. |
   214	| `build/reports/code-review/code-review-<timestamp>.md` | Timestamped Markdown audit copy. |
   215	| `build/reports/code-review/verification-<timestamp>.log` | Complete build and test evidence used by the reviewer. |
   216	| `build/reports/jacoco/test/html/index.html` | Human-readable test coverage report. |
   217	
   218	Architects use `latest.html` and its JaCoCo link. Detailed Gradle output remains in the timestamped
   219	verification log for audit or troubleshooting and is intentionally omitted from the architect report.
   220	
   221	The `build/` directory is intentionally ignored by Git because reports are generated results tied to
   222	one checkout, branch, and execution time. Anyone who clones the repository can reproduce current
   223	reports by running `./code-review`. Reports are not automatically visible in the GitHub source tree.
   224	
   225	### Understanding the decision
   226	
   227	The HTML report explains whether the work is ready for normal architect approval, needs follow-up,
   228	requires changes, must be rejected, or lacks enough evidence. Serious verified findings and build,
   229	test, or coverage failures override a high numerical score. The standards percentage means the
   230	percentage of verified applicable controls satisfied; it is not the percentage of source code that
   231	is correct, secure, tested, or production-ready. Each category shows its score, baseline, evidence
   232	coverage, result, and a concise architect summary without repeating raw control counts.
   233	
   234	### Troubleshooting the review command
   235	
   236	- **`codex: command not found`:** install Codex, open a new terminal, and run `codex --version`.
   237	- **Codex is not authenticated:** run `codex login status`, followed by `codex login` if required.
   238	- **Java toolchain not found:** install JDK 23 and verify it with `java -version` and
   239	  `./gradlew --version`.
   240	- **`Permission denied: ./code-review`:** run `chmod +x code-review gradlew` on macOS/Linux.
   241	- **Staged mode reports no changes:** stage the intended files with `git add` first.
   242	- **Automatic review requires a named Git branch:** check out `main` for a complete repository
   243	  review or a feature branch for a change-only review.
   244	- **Branch comparison requires a clean working tree:** commit the feature changes, or stage the
   245	  intended changes and run `./code-review --staged`.
   246	- **No committed branch changes were found:** confirm the feature commit exists with
   247	  `git log --oneline main..HEAD`.
   248	- **Base revision does not exist:** fetch it yourself if appropriate, then confirm with
   249	  `git rev-parse --verify main` or use the correct local base revision.
   250	- **No report appears:** inspect the terminal error and the preserved timestamped transcript under
   251	  `build/reports/code-review/`. The launcher returns a nonzero status for execution failures and
   252	  preserves partial output when possible.
   253	- **Windows:** run `./code-review` from Git Bash or WSL. Use `gradlew.bat` when running Gradle
   254	  directly from Command Prompt or PowerShell.
   255	
   256	## Build and test commands
   257	
   258	```shell
   259	./gradlew clean test
   260	./gradlew build
   261	./gradlew jacocoTestCoverageVerification jacocoTestReport
   262	```
   263	
   264	## Run locally
   265	
   266	```shell
   267	./gradlew bootRun
   268	```
   269	
   270	The default `local` profile starts the API at `http://localhost:8080/api/orders`. Health information
   271	is public at `http://localhost:8080/actuator/health`; the local-only H2 console is available at
   272	`http://localhost:8080/h2-console` with JDBC URL
   273	`jdbc:h2:mem:orders`, user `sa`, and an empty password.
   274	
   275	Read endpoints are public. Write endpoints require HTTP Basic authentication. The local
   276	demonstration account is `reviewer` with password `review-demo-only`; override it before a shared
   277	demo by setting `ORDER_ADMIN_PASSWORD`. This fallback is not used by the production profile.
   278	
   279	Three demonstration orders are loaded into the in-memory H2 database at startup from
   280	`src/main/resources/data.sql`. Changes persist while the application is running and reset to
   281	the sample data after a restart. Flyway creates the schema from
   282	`src/main/resources/db/migration/V1__create_customer_orders.sql`; Hibernate only validates it.
   283	
   284	## Production profile
   285	
   286	The `prod` profile uses PostgreSQL, disables seed data and the H2 console, validates the schema,
   287	and acts as a stateless OAuth 2.0 JWT resource server. It reads deployment configuration from the
   288	environment:
   289	
   290	```shell
   291	export SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod
   292	export DB_URL='jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/orders'
   293	export DB_USERNAME='orders_app'
   294	export DB_PASSWORD='replace-me'
   295	export OIDC_ISSUER_URI='https://identity.example.com/realms/orders'
   296	export OIDC_AUDIENCE='smart-code-review'
   297	./gradlew bootRun
   298	```
   299	
   300	Flyway applies versioned migrations before JPA starts. Store the environment values in the
   301	deployment platform's secret manager; do not commit them. The identity provider must issue a
   302	`roles` claim containing `ORDER_READER` for read access, `ORDER_ADMIN` for read/write access, or
   303	`OPERATIONS` for non-health actuator access. It must also include the value configured by
   304	`OIDC_AUDIENCE` in the token's `aud` claim. HTTP Basic authentication exists only in the local
   305	demonstration profile.
   306	
   307	Production liveness and readiness endpoints are available at `/actuator/health/liveness` and
   308	`/actuator/health/readiness`. Readiness includes database health because every order operation
   309	depends on PostgreSQL; liveness remains process-focused to avoid restart loops during database
   310	outages. Both endpoints are safe for platform probes. Application logs include the sanitized
   311	`X-Correlation-Id` value in their logging context.
   312	
   313	## API
   314	
   315	| Method | Path | Description |
   316	| --- | --- | --- |
   317	| `POST` | `/api/orders` | Create an order |
   318	| `GET` | `/api/orders?page=0&size=20` | List orders, ordered by ID; page size is limited to 100 |
   319	| `GET` | `/api/orders/{id}` | Retrieve an order |
   320	| `PUT` | `/api/orders/{id}` | Replace an order |
   321	| `DELETE` | `/api/orders/{id}` | Delete an order |
   322	
   323	Example request:
   324	
   325	```shell
   326	curl -i -u reviewer:${ORDER_ADMIN_PASSWORD:-review-demo-only} http://localhost:8080/api/orders \
   327	  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
   328	  -d '{
   329	    "customerName": "Grace Hopper",
   330	    "productName": "Mechanical Keyboard",
   331	    "quantity": 2,
   332	    "unitPrice": 75.50,
   333	    "status": "PENDING"
   334	  }'
   335	```
   336	
   337	Valid statuses are `PENDING`, `CONFIRMED`, `SHIPPED`, `DELIVERED`, and `CANCELLED`.
   338	
   339	`POST` creates a new order each time and is intentionally non-idempotent; clients must not retry
   340	it blindly. Create, retrieve, and update responses include an `ETag` containing the entity version.
   341	Clients must send that value in `If-Match` when updating or deleting; an intervening change produces
   342	`412 Precondition Failed`, while an overlapping JPA optimistic-lock conflict produces `409 Conflict`.
   343	Validation and known failures use one stable `ApiError` response,
   344	while unexpected exception details are logged server-side and are not returned to clients. Every
   345	response includes `X-Correlation-Id`; a safe caller-supplied value is propagated, otherwise the
   346	service creates one and places it in the logging context.
   347	
   348	## Package structure
   349	
   350	The root package is `com.manjusha.smartcodereview`. The application uses package-by-feature
   351	with explicit layers inside the `order` feature:
   352	
   353	```text
   354	com.manjusha.smartcodereview
   355	├── config
   356	├── order
   357	│   ├── controller
   358	│   ├── dto
   359	│   ├── entity
   360	│   ├── repository
   361	│   └── service
   362	└── exception
   363	```
   364	
   365	This keeps the Order feature cohesive while making controller, domain, persistence, and
   366	business-service boundaries explicit. Cross-cutting security configuration lives in `config`, and
   367	HTTP error handling lives in `exception`.
